WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.876 --> 00:00:04.212
- I don't think I ever became competitive

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:07.632
actually until I was 88

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:09.884
(Rich laughing)

00:00:09.884 --> 00:00:13.013
and I got first place in what they call

00:00:13.013 --> 00:00:16.599
the single-age running.

00:00:17.976 --> 00:00:21.062
I'm 22 years older than the average person

00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:24.399
who dies in this country,
and it's been a pleasure.

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:29.988
For my age, I'm practically number one.

00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:32.657
(both laughing)

00:00:35.285 --> 00:00:36.119
- Hey everybody.

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:38.079
Welcome to the podcast, my podcast,

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:41.833
the show where we go deep on a
really wide variety of stuff,

00:00:41.833 --> 00:00:46.421
stuff that I think matters,
including health, fitness,

00:00:46.421 --> 00:00:47.714
nutrition, what else?

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:51.468
Longevity, what it means to
pursue a life of meaning?

00:00:51.468 --> 00:00:54.804
Which are all subjects we're
privileged to explore today

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:58.516
through the lived
experience of a centenarian,

00:00:58.516 --> 00:01:01.478
a human who has literally
been walking the Earth

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:03.813
for a full 100 years.

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:06.566
Which is, of course,
rare, it's really unique

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.526
and just an amazing privilege

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:10.403
to be able to talk to him today.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:11.821
His name is Mike Fremont.

00:01:11.821 --> 00:01:13.448
Mike is a retired engineer,

00:01:13.448 --> 00:01:16.201
turned climate activist and athlete

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:20.246
who in addition to being
pretty darn with it

00:01:20.246 --> 00:01:22.457
holds, prepare yourself, get this,

00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:25.627
just an absolute slew of age group

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:27.253
world records in running,

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:32.175
including the fastest recorded
marathon for an 88-year-old

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:35.595
for a 90-year-old and a 91-year-old,

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:37.055
if I got that right.

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:38.848
At the ripe young age of 96,

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:40.934
Mike set the American one mile record

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.812
for the 95 to 99 age group.

00:01:43.812 --> 00:01:47.649
And as a lifelong canoe racer at 99,

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:49.150
he was the oldest person to race

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.819
the Canoe National Championships.

00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:53.363
And Mike's still out
there every single day,

00:01:53.363 --> 00:01:54.656
just getting after it.

00:01:54.656 --> 00:01:56.366
So how does he do it?

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:58.827
What is Mike's secret?

00:01:58.827 --> 00:02:00.703
Well, this conversation is my attempt

00:02:00.703 --> 00:02:04.332
to extract his testimony and his counsel

00:02:04.332 --> 00:02:07.544
for younger generations on
what he's learned about life,

00:02:07.544 --> 00:02:10.421
what he's learned about
longevity, vitality,

00:02:10.421 --> 00:02:14.259
purpose, fitness, setting
world records and diet.

00:02:14.259 --> 00:02:17.053
Because for Mike, it's
kind of all about diet,

00:02:17.053 --> 00:02:19.681
specifically the whole
food plant-based diet

00:02:19.681 --> 00:02:22.308
that he adopted 30 years ago,

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.227
something he did in the wake of receiving

00:02:24.227 --> 00:02:27.689
a colon cancer diagnosis
that he really credits

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:30.525
as fueling his training.

00:02:30.525 --> 00:02:32.277
It's what keeps him spry.

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:35.196
It's what informs his climate activism.

00:02:35.196 --> 00:02:37.574
And in his words, "really is the thing

00:02:37.574 --> 00:02:39.993
"that has allowed him to thrive decades

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.871
"beyond social expectations."

00:02:42.871 --> 00:02:45.999
It's not often you get the
opportunity to spend time

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:47.917
with a centenarian.

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:50.378
And I really think our
culture fails pretty miserably

00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:52.630
when it comes to appreciating our elders.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:56.009
So I really loved having him on the show.

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:59.137
Shout out to Mike's running
buddy elite ultra-marathoner

00:02:59.137 --> 00:03:01.139
and friend of the podcast, Harvey Lewis

00:03:02.056 --> 00:03:04.392
for making this podcast happen.

00:03:04.392 --> 00:03:06.269
I consider Mike a brand new friend,

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:08.354
and I'm just really
proud to share his voice

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:09.439
with all of you today.

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:12.984
So please hit that subscribe
button and let's do it.

00:03:12.984 --> 00:03:15.403
This is me and Mike Fremont.

00:03:17.822 --> 00:03:19.657
Well, Mike, it's a pleasure to meet you.

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:21.784
It's an honor to talk to you today.

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:23.661
It's a rare thing

00:03:24.662 --> 00:03:27.665
that anybody gets to
sit down with somebody

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:29.959
who has experienced so much life as you

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:34.422
and I just can't wait to
hear more about your life.

00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:37.425
I guess the first obvious
question is, how does it,

00:03:37.425 --> 00:03:38.259
how are you doing?

00:03:38.259 --> 00:03:39.761
Like, how do you feel?

00:03:39.761 --> 00:03:42.013
What is it like to be 100?

00:03:43.306 --> 00:03:47.185
- These, believe it or not,
are the very best years

00:03:47.185 --> 00:03:48.102
of my life.

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:49.979
No question.

00:03:49.979 --> 00:03:51.439
- Why is that.

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:54.317
- Things that I've
worked for and worked on

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:58.029
have blossomed out.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:00.698
I'm still here.

00:04:02.116 --> 00:04:04.994
I can still run, so to speak.

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:09.832
For my age, I'm practically number one.

00:04:09.832 --> 00:04:12.168
(both laughing)
- Yeah.

00:04:12.168 --> 00:04:14.545
By sheer process of elimination.

00:04:14.545 --> 00:04:18.466
But also, I mean, it's amazing
that you still get out there,

00:04:18.466 --> 00:04:21.302
you're still running,
you're so engaged with life.

00:04:22.971 --> 00:04:25.932
I have to imagine that's
a big part of the secret,

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:29.102
just being engaged with
life and finding purpose

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:30.687
in the things that interest you.

00:04:31.896 --> 00:04:36.359
- Well, it turned out that
I made a wise decision

00:04:38.194 --> 00:04:40.571
when I was faced with an operation.

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:47.620
And I hold that largely
responsible for all the records

00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:51.332
that I set in old age.

00:04:53.167 --> 00:04:54.794
No question in my mind.

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:59.799
Absolutely, it is diet that
has determined my existence,

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:04.846
my continued existence
and my beautiful health.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:07.557
- So tell me more about that.

00:05:07.557 --> 00:05:11.477
You were 70 when you were
diagnosed with colon cancer

00:05:11.477 --> 00:05:12.770
- Yeah 69, actually.
- 69.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:15.273
So yeah. Maybe explain that a little bit.

00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:21.404
- Yeah. I had a polyp, P-O-L-Y-P,

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:28.077
in one of my nether parts,
and the doctor I went to

00:05:29.954 --> 00:05:34.000
sent me to the Cleveland
Clinic for a proper diagnosis.

00:05:35.335 --> 00:05:39.672
And they put a television
set connection into me,

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:42.175
so I could see what was going on,

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:48.181
and they said that this
tumor that they saw

00:05:50.099 --> 00:05:52.602
had metastasized,

00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:56.606
which means it had taken root
in different parts of my body,

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.108
the lymphatic system,

00:06:00.318 --> 00:06:02.820
and that even if they took the tumor out,

00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:06.491
they would have to go
after those metastases.

00:06:08.993 --> 00:06:11.913
And at the time I had,

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:17.293
by that time received a book
from my son in California,

00:06:17.293 --> 00:06:21.839
that I'll see probably
tomorrow, (chuckles)

00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:24.258
who had sent me a book called

00:06:24.258 --> 00:06:29.263
"The Cancer Prevention
Diet" by Michio Kushi,

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:33.851
a Japanese chap who
settled here and was in

00:06:33.851 --> 00:06:38.856
Becket, Massachusetts with his entourage.

00:06:38.856 --> 00:06:43.861
And I called him to ask him
why he sent me the book?

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.280
He said, "You may need it someday."

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:48.991
So, of course, I didn't read it

00:06:50.326 --> 00:06:52.745
until I was diagnosed,

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:54.705
at which point I devoured it.

00:06:56.207 --> 00:07:00.086
And I went cold turkey on what
is now known as a vegan diet.

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:06.300
At that time, it was a macrobiotic diet.

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.512
I think it was even beginning
to be accepted a little bit

00:07:09.512 --> 00:07:11.806
by people in California. (chuckles)

00:07:11.806 --> 00:07:16.018
- Sure. So essentially
for the last 30 years

00:07:16.018 --> 00:07:18.438
you've been eating a very strict whole

00:07:18.438 --> 00:07:20.440
plant-based diet.
- 100%.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.819
100%, no exceptions, cold turkey.

00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:26.612
- And what happened to?

00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:30.491
You get the diagnosis, excuse
me, from the Cleveland Clinic,

00:07:30.491 --> 00:07:33.911
did they wanna put you in chemotherapy,

00:07:33.911 --> 00:07:35.997
or did you do chemotherapy
or what happened?

00:07:35.997 --> 00:07:40.793
- They said, if you're not operated on

00:07:41.919 --> 00:07:43.963
you have three months to live.

00:07:45.173 --> 00:07:48.176
That was the other side of
the equation, I thought.

00:07:48.176 --> 00:07:52.847
So I decided after reading the book,

00:07:52.847 --> 00:07:54.932
I decided I would try the diet.

00:07:56.559 --> 00:08:00.855
And I said, I'll check with
you guys from time to time,

00:08:00.855 --> 00:08:03.441
which I didn't do because I,

00:08:04.317 --> 00:08:07.361
two weeks after going on the diet,

00:08:08.529 --> 00:08:13.159
I realized that various
things were happening to me

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:16.162
that I hadn't anticipated,

00:08:16.162 --> 00:08:19.332
such as arthritis in the back of my neck,

00:08:19.332 --> 00:08:21.250
arthritis in this shoulder,

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:26.047
arthritis in these
fingers had disappeared.

00:08:26.047 --> 00:08:29.509
No explanation, no problem.

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:35.139
Furthermore, my hands and
lips used to be chapped

00:08:35.139 --> 00:08:37.141
from the time I was a little kid.

00:08:38.142 --> 00:08:39.769
That disappeared.
- Wow.

00:08:39.769 --> 00:08:40.978
- It disappeared.

00:08:42.146 --> 00:08:44.857
The only thing I might have had,

00:08:44.857 --> 00:08:49.862
which by that time I was
old enough to have outgrown

00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:55.743
was asthma, which is what
kept me out of the military,

00:08:56.577 --> 00:08:59.539
which had kept me out of
school for a month once.

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:04.293
- Was this for, so you were born,

00:09:05.753 --> 00:09:08.881
I assume you were born in like 1922?

00:09:08.881 --> 00:09:09.715
- Exactly. Yeah.

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:13.427
- So you would've been prime
candidate for World War II?

00:09:13.427 --> 00:09:14.303
- Exactly.

00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:19.183
So half of my class was
in uniform at college,

00:09:20.309 --> 00:09:22.436
In uniform, and they had their

00:09:24.438 --> 00:09:28.734
educational experience there too,

00:09:30.319 --> 00:09:32.655
and I regretted I
couldn't be part of them.

00:09:33.656 --> 00:09:37.910
Anyway, that diet

00:09:39.996 --> 00:09:43.583
had so much going for it I marvel

00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:49.922
that big business hasn't
grabbed it and run with it

00:09:50.798 --> 00:09:52.633
after all these years.

00:09:53.551 --> 00:09:56.554
People used to look at me
strangely when I'd say,

00:09:56.554 --> 00:09:58.681
"No, I don't eat meat.

00:09:58.681 --> 00:10:00.641
"No, I don't drink milk.

00:10:00.641 --> 00:10:02.810
"No eggs, no eggs." (chuckles)

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.021
- Yeah. Well, it has, I mean,

00:10:05.021 --> 00:10:08.566
there is big business now
in that it is much more

00:10:08.566 --> 00:10:12.320
widely acceptable and adopted.
- It is.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:13.946
- And there are lots of
companies making products

00:10:13.946 --> 00:10:15.156
for this lifestyle.

00:10:15.156 --> 00:10:18.409
But you are correct,
and it is interesting.

00:10:18.409 --> 00:10:23.289
So your arthritis started to disappear,

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:25.207
you knew something was
going on inside you,

00:10:25.207 --> 00:10:27.126
but did you have a sense
that this was helping

00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:28.377
with your cancer?

00:10:31.213 --> 00:10:36.177
- I'm no doctor, I can't
get into that science,

00:10:38.846 --> 00:10:41.265
but several of these diseases that we have

00:10:46.228 --> 00:10:51.192
are truly associated
with eating certain meats

00:10:52.943 --> 00:10:55.154
and what other categories there are.

00:10:56.197 --> 00:10:59.950
- So you had adopted this diet
and you were eating this way

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:01.410
for a couple years.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:05.206
And then at some point you had
the tumor surgically removed,

00:11:05.206 --> 00:11:07.333
am I right?
- It began to bleed again.

00:11:07.333 --> 00:11:10.378
I knew I had to be operated
for the basic tumor.

00:11:11.545 --> 00:11:13.756
I was operated by a doctor in Dayton,

00:11:13.756 --> 00:11:15.132
who'd been in the army.

00:11:15.132 --> 00:11:16.884
He'd been an army doctor

00:11:18.177 --> 00:11:20.888
who was specialized in
that kind of operation.

00:11:23.349 --> 00:11:24.809
And he told Marilyn

00:11:26.435 --> 00:11:29.772
after the operation that
he'd looked in 35 places

00:11:29.772 --> 00:11:34.485
for metastasis and found none, none.

00:11:36.153 --> 00:11:37.613
35 places.

00:11:38.572 --> 00:11:41.867
So what I had done by simply changing diet

00:11:43.786 --> 00:11:45.579
was to kill the metastases

00:11:47.498 --> 00:11:50.209
that would possibly have killed me.

00:11:51.377 --> 00:11:54.422
I found out subsequently,
quite a bit subsequently,

00:11:54.422 --> 00:11:56.757
as a matter of fact, a
matter of months ago,

00:11:59.802 --> 00:12:04.807
that there are something
like 52,000 people a year

00:12:05.057 --> 00:12:07.727
who die of colorectal cancer.

00:12:09.979 --> 00:12:14.984
90% of them die of the metastases.

00:12:16.235 --> 00:12:17.653
90%.

00:12:17.653 --> 00:12:18.779
- Yeah. That's amazing.

00:12:18.779 --> 00:12:21.615
And it never, after you
had the tumor removed,

00:12:21.615 --> 00:12:25.286
there was no, you were in
remission and it never came back.

00:12:26.954 --> 00:12:31.959
- Well, I feel that I can
counsel, actually counsel people

00:12:31.959 --> 00:12:35.337
who had this particular
disease. (chuckles)

00:12:35.337 --> 00:12:37.882
Say, "Try this. Try this." (chuckles)

00:12:37.882 --> 00:12:42.303
- Right. And where does the running begin?

00:12:43.387 --> 00:12:44.221
- Running?

00:12:45.973 --> 00:12:49.101
I was 36, I had three little children,

00:12:50.519 --> 00:12:55.483
and my first wife, and she
died of my brain hemorrhage

00:12:55.483 --> 00:12:57.485
when our daughter was two weeks old.

00:12:59.653 --> 00:13:03.949
And I had started a business a year before

00:13:05.159 --> 00:13:08.996
and I was all alone and decided,

00:13:12.875 --> 00:13:16.921
was stressed and I lived on a dam,

00:13:18.672 --> 00:13:22.885
and I used to run across
that dam, which was level,

00:13:24.929 --> 00:13:26.597
every day after work.

00:13:27.556 --> 00:13:29.558
And it was very rewarding,

00:13:29.558 --> 00:13:32.269
it was better than two martinis.

00:13:32.269 --> 00:13:34.313
(both laughing)

00:13:34.313 --> 00:13:37.066
- But not to be competitive at that time?

00:13:38.275 --> 00:13:39.485
- No thought of it.

00:13:40.486 --> 00:13:43.697
Then someone said, "Why
don't you get in a race?"

00:13:43.697 --> 00:13:48.702
And I said, "Me! Like, I'm
50 years old, (chuckles)

00:13:50.329 --> 00:13:52.456
"36 years old or whatever was it."

00:13:53.624 --> 00:13:56.126
But I kept running. I enjoyed it.

00:13:56.126 --> 00:14:00.339
Used to go to the beach,
whenever we went to the beach,

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:03.634
which is not in Cincinnati, of course.

00:14:04.510 --> 00:14:07.972
So running became a
little part of my life.

00:14:10.432 --> 00:14:12.226
And then they got me in a race

00:14:14.436 --> 00:14:16.605
and I did, okay.

00:14:16.605 --> 00:14:19.650
I didn't come in last (laughs)

00:14:20.651 --> 00:14:23.612
They said, "Are you gonna try for Boston?"

00:14:23.612 --> 00:14:27.491
I said, "I have to run a
marathon first." (laughs)

00:14:27.491 --> 00:14:31.662
So I ran a marathon and
didn't qualify for Boston.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:37.626
So I ran a few more
marathons and I qualified.

00:14:37.626 --> 00:14:41.714
Then I did my first Boston and I thought,

00:14:41.714 --> 00:14:43.215
"Wow, you can actually run."

00:14:44.592 --> 00:14:46.635
- Yeah. So it's around 60

00:14:46.635 --> 00:14:49.430
where you start getting
competitive though,

00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:51.473
as I understand, is that correct?

00:14:51.473 --> 00:14:52.766
- Not really.

00:14:52.766 --> 00:14:55.269
I don't think I ever became competitive,

00:14:55.269 --> 00:14:58.188
actually, until I was 88.

00:14:59.523 --> 00:15:00.733
(both laughing)

00:15:00.733 --> 00:15:04.236
I ran a marathon when I
was 88 and I got first.

00:15:04.236 --> 00:15:09.241
First place in history
in what they call the

00:15:14.788 --> 00:15:19.627
world single-age running category.

00:15:19.627 --> 00:15:21.962
I brought you some sheets
of that to show you,

00:15:21.962 --> 00:15:23.505
if you hadn't seen it.
- Sure.

00:15:23.505 --> 00:15:27.426
Yeah. So at 88 you set the
single-age world record

00:15:27.426 --> 00:15:29.136
for the marathon.

00:15:29.136 --> 00:15:31.931
And then at 90 you do it again,

00:15:31.931 --> 00:15:35.059
the single-age world
record for the marathon.

00:15:36.101 --> 00:15:38.646
And at 90, you also set
the single-age world record

00:15:38.646 --> 00:15:40.606
for the half marathon.

00:15:40.606 --> 00:15:43.400
And at 91, the single-age world record

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:44.818
for the half marathon.

00:15:44.818 --> 00:15:45.653
- That's right.

00:15:45.653 --> 00:15:50.074
- So you have One, two,
four world records,

00:15:50.074 --> 00:15:52.785
and you have a whole slew of five-year

00:15:52.785 --> 00:15:55.079
age group records going,

00:15:55.079 --> 00:15:56.789
there's too many to even keep track.

00:15:56.789 --> 00:15:58.958
- I didn't even realize that at the time,

00:15:59.959 --> 00:16:02.086
there's not much competition out there.

00:16:02.086 --> 00:16:06.090
There hasn't been because
people don't understand

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:09.760
the system, how your system
works and contributes

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:12.137
to you being able to do these things.

00:16:12.137 --> 00:16:13.389
- So talk about that,

00:16:13.389 --> 00:16:16.976
I wanna hear more about
how to do this thing.

00:16:16.976 --> 00:16:19.311
So maybe explain how you do it.

00:16:19.311 --> 00:16:24.024
Like, how are you able
to not just run marathons

00:16:24.024 --> 00:16:25.985
and half marathons in your late 80s

00:16:25.985 --> 00:16:27.611
and over the course of your 90s,

00:16:27.611 --> 00:16:29.405
but also set world records.

00:16:29.405 --> 00:16:32.658
Like, what is the secret
to longevity here?

00:16:32.658 --> 00:16:33.784
We talked about diet,

00:16:33.784 --> 00:16:37.454
but also what is your fitness routine?

00:16:37.454 --> 00:16:42.418
- The longevity part of it is
the interesting part, I think.

00:16:44.169 --> 00:16:47.589
And the Japanese are credited

00:16:48.465 --> 00:16:53.429
with living six years longer
on average than Americans.

00:16:55.597 --> 00:16:58.225
That's a huge amount.
- Yeah.

00:16:58.225 --> 00:17:00.102
- But here I've already,

00:17:00.102 --> 00:17:03.897
I'm 22 years older than
the average person who dies

00:17:03.897 --> 00:17:05.065
in this country.

00:17:06.483 --> 00:17:07.901
And it's been a pleasure.

00:17:11.739 --> 00:17:15.909
And it's simply because of what I eat,

00:17:15.909 --> 00:17:20.539
which is not that specifically
precision designed

00:17:25.878 --> 00:17:27.546
to make me an expert.

00:17:27.546 --> 00:17:29.339
I'm not an expert on it.

00:17:29.339 --> 00:17:31.091
I know what works for me,

00:17:32.342 --> 00:17:35.888
and I've never had any question about it.

00:17:35.888 --> 00:17:39.767
But I just don't do bad things in eating.

00:17:41.143 --> 00:17:43.479
- Tell me exactly what it is that you eat,

00:17:43.479 --> 00:17:45.856
like a day in the life of food for you?

00:17:45.856 --> 00:17:47.900
Like, what are your meals?

00:17:47.900 --> 00:17:52.905
- Well, tomorrow or today, (chuckles)

00:17:54.698 --> 00:17:58.619
I had some oatmeal that
had a few blueberries

00:17:58.619 --> 00:17:59.453
on the top of it, and
then some sweet dollop

00:17:59.453 --> 00:18:04.416
or syrup or something,

00:18:04.416 --> 00:18:07.753
little teeny thing. (chuckles)

00:18:07.753 --> 00:18:11.757
And I had a cup, that was about this high,

00:18:11.757 --> 00:18:15.928
filled with fruits, actually with berries,

00:18:15.928 --> 00:18:20.933
strawberries and
blackberries and blueberries.

00:18:24.353 --> 00:18:26.855
And one other thing

00:18:26.855 --> 00:18:29.191
might have been a piece
of mango or something.

00:18:32.236 --> 00:18:33.695
It was $7 at the motel,

00:18:33.695 --> 00:18:35.906
which is a very high price, of course.

00:18:35.906 --> 00:18:37.116
- (laughs) Welcome to Los Angeles.

00:18:37.116 --> 00:18:40.661
- Wouldn't be high price
if it was alcohol. (laughs)

00:18:40.661 --> 00:18:44.206
- And what would a lunch and
a dinner look like for you?

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:49.253
- Oh, I might have something as prosaic as

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:55.968
half a can of black beans,

00:18:55.968 --> 00:18:58.137
or kidney beans or garbanzo beans

00:19:02.766 --> 00:19:04.518
with a little bit of

00:19:05.853 --> 00:19:06.687
tamari salty sauce-
- Tamari.

00:19:07.771 --> 00:19:09.731
- Just a few drops of it,

00:19:09.731 --> 00:19:11.942
and then I'll put the other
half away in the ice box

00:19:11.942 --> 00:19:14.361
and eat it the next day.

00:19:14.361 --> 00:19:16.363
So I try to get beans every day.

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:26.165
I might do some broccoli flowers,

00:19:27.833 --> 00:19:30.210
which I use a little bit of ketchup with

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:33.046
to make 'em taste like something.

00:19:33.046 --> 00:19:35.174
- Yeah. (laughs)

00:19:35.174 --> 00:19:40.179
Okay. So pretty basic close
to their natural state.

00:19:40.929 --> 00:19:42.306
Sorry to interrupt the flow.

00:19:42.306 --> 00:19:43.932
We'll be right back with more awesome.

