00:21:12.521 --> 00:21:15.357
All right, let's get back to the show.

00:21:15.357 --> 00:21:18.110
So what about the fitness routine?

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:21.405
What is the secret to how
you've been able to continue

00:21:21.405 --> 00:21:25.033
running into your 90s and now at 100?

00:21:25.033 --> 00:21:28.620
- I was able to retire at
the age of 88. (laughs)

00:21:29.788 --> 00:21:32.374
- So low stress, free time.

00:21:33.375 --> 00:21:35.210
- Stress also kills.

00:21:36.336 --> 00:21:39.756
And if you can keep
your life from distress,

00:21:39.756 --> 00:21:43.176
as well as stress, you're very fortunate.

00:21:44.136 --> 00:21:47.973
But diet and stress are the
two things you can kill.

00:21:47.973 --> 00:21:49.599
Definitely can kill.

00:21:50.684 --> 00:21:55.689
- But tell me about your
daily fitness routine

00:21:55.897 --> 00:21:59.192
and how you've been able
to stay active and running.

00:21:59.192 --> 00:22:01.486
Like how do you keep it up?

00:22:01.486 --> 00:22:04.906
- Well, actually, I run in
a place called Sharon Woods,

00:22:04.906 --> 00:22:09.870
which is a county park
five and a half miles

00:22:10.579 --> 00:22:11.747
from where I live.

00:22:12.748 --> 00:22:14.207
And it has hills,

00:22:16.126 --> 00:22:19.379
and it has a gravel path,

00:22:19.379 --> 00:22:23.925
and it has blacktop path all together,

00:22:25.635 --> 00:22:28.347
and one circle is five miles.

00:22:31.016 --> 00:22:33.226
And the same people come
out there year after year,

00:22:33.226 --> 00:22:35.729
all the time, you expect to see them,

00:22:35.729 --> 00:22:36.980
you make friends of 'em.

00:22:39.983 --> 00:22:41.109
And I do that.

00:22:41.985 --> 00:22:46.990
My routine had been three times
a week, Monday, not Monday,

00:22:47.949 --> 00:22:51.661
Sunday, Tuesday, and Thursday

00:22:51.661 --> 00:22:55.248
I would run 10 miles until I was 98.

00:22:55.248 --> 00:22:56.583
- Yeah. Wow.

00:22:56.583 --> 00:22:59.419
- I decided it was taking too
long for me to run 10 miles

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:03.423
(laughs) with people I had grown to know

00:23:03.423 --> 00:23:05.550
over how many years was it?

00:23:05.550 --> 00:23:09.888
It was about, let me see,

00:23:09.888 --> 00:23:13.892
we set up a little group in 1979.

00:23:18.397 --> 00:23:23.276
Yeah, yeah. That's 21 in 40 years.

00:23:23.276 --> 00:23:25.028
- That's quite a long time.

00:23:25.028 --> 00:23:27.531
10 miles, three times a week.

00:23:27.531 --> 00:23:28.740
10 miles, three times a week.

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:32.452
- That was 30 miles, 10
miles three times a week.

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:34.704
- And what is it like now?

00:23:36.039 --> 00:23:38.375
- It's five miles three times a week.

00:23:40.001 --> 00:23:42.045
- And do you do any, I know canoeing.

00:23:42.045 --> 00:23:43.380
I wanna talk about the canoeing,

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:46.091
but do you do any other kind of exercises?

00:23:47.717 --> 00:23:48.593
- Up to this year

00:23:49.594 --> 00:23:51.805
I've been a canoe racer

00:23:52.722 --> 00:23:57.227
on a lake that's three miles away

00:23:57.227 --> 00:24:00.063
and they refused to let the lake

00:24:00.063 --> 00:24:05.068
be used, the county park lake,
by people who might drown,

00:24:07.362 --> 00:24:12.242
you know, people self-propelled
canoes or kayaks.

00:24:13.785 --> 00:24:18.790
They even kept Roland Mullen,
who was a Olympic champion,

00:24:19.374 --> 00:24:24.129
three times, in canoe
racing in the Olympics.

00:24:25.547 --> 00:24:27.132
They wouldn't let him

00:24:27.132 --> 00:24:27.966
practice on it.
- They wouldn't want him,

00:24:27.966 --> 00:24:29.843
they were worried about him drowning?

00:24:30.844 --> 00:24:33.513
(both laughing)

00:24:35.140 --> 00:24:38.477
- What we did was to
scare the park district

00:24:39.603 --> 00:24:41.938
because that had lawyers
and their insurance people

00:24:41.938 --> 00:24:44.232
and said, "Don't let people do that,

00:24:44.232 --> 00:24:48.820
"they'll drown and they'll
sue and you'll have to pay."

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:51.823
But we said, "We pay for these parks.

00:24:51.823 --> 00:24:54.034
"We have a right to use them."

00:24:54.034 --> 00:24:58.455
So they finally said, "Okay
in 2000, we'll open 'em up."

00:24:58.455 --> 00:24:59.289
And they did.

00:25:00.373 --> 00:25:02.918
So I was one of the first guys out there

00:25:02.918 --> 00:25:04.419
with my racing canoe.

00:25:05.795 --> 00:25:08.256
And another guy in an
old canoe came up to me,

00:25:08.256 --> 00:25:10.842
and he says, "What kind of canoe is that?"

00:25:10.842 --> 00:25:14.012
And I described it and I
said, "You wanna try it?"

00:25:14.012 --> 00:25:15.722
And he said, "Yeah."

00:25:17.349 --> 00:25:19.601
So we exchanged canoes and he tried it.

00:25:21.186 --> 00:25:25.357
The next day he took his
pickup truck and drove it

00:25:25.357 --> 00:25:28.693
to Western Maryland, bought the same canoe

00:25:28.693 --> 00:25:30.946
and drove it back the same day.

00:25:30.946 --> 00:25:31.780
- Oh wow.

00:25:32.906 --> 00:25:35.283
And he became your canoeing buddy?

00:25:35.283 --> 00:25:37.869
- He became my canoeing buddy.

00:25:37.869 --> 00:25:39.788
And then we attracted another guy

00:25:39.788 --> 00:25:42.958
because we're out there plugging away.

00:25:44.292 --> 00:25:46.211
And then I said, you know,

00:25:47.712 --> 00:25:50.382
"We ought to start a group here."

00:25:51.508 --> 00:25:54.678
He at the time was 13
years younger than me.

00:25:55.929 --> 00:25:57.430
That was 2000.

00:25:57.430 --> 00:26:00.517
So I was what, 78?

00:26:02.185 --> 00:26:04.396
Yeah. He was 13 years younger.

00:26:04.396 --> 00:26:05.647
He was 65.

00:26:08.525 --> 00:26:10.694
I said, "You're gonna be the TAPIOCA

00:26:10.694 --> 00:26:12.153
of this organization.

00:26:13.321 --> 00:26:15.156
He said, "What the hell is that?"

00:26:16.116 --> 00:26:21.121
I said, it's the temporary
acting provisional interim

00:26:22.205 --> 00:26:25.584
orchestrator of coalition activities.

00:26:25.584 --> 00:26:28.253
(both laughing)

00:26:30.839 --> 00:26:31.673
- There you go.

00:26:33.091 --> 00:26:34.467
- That's quite a handle.

00:26:34.467 --> 00:26:35.719
- It's a lot of pressure.

00:26:37.095 --> 00:26:39.514
- And he said, "What should
we call this outfit?"

00:26:40.473 --> 00:26:43.643
I said, "Why don't we
call it the EPA?" (laughs)

00:26:46.146 --> 00:26:50.734
So he said, "That's the
Elderly Paddlers Association."

00:26:50.734 --> 00:26:53.486
(both laughing)

00:26:53.486 --> 00:26:54.863
- There you go.

00:26:54.863 --> 00:26:56.865
- So we attracted a few more.

00:26:56.865 --> 00:27:00.910
We probably got about 12 all together,

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:05.915
and Marilyn, as one of them
who come out on Monday,

00:27:07.250 --> 00:27:12.255
Wednesday and Friday,
that's 07:30 in the morning

00:27:12.672 --> 00:27:15.800
before the motorboats go
out there, before it's

00:27:19.971 --> 00:27:22.641
early enough to be daylight.

00:27:22.641 --> 00:27:25.393
I mean, late enough to be daylight.

00:27:25.393 --> 00:27:27.812
- Right. And are you still doing that now?

00:27:27.812 --> 00:27:28.647
- Yeah.
- You are.

00:27:28.647 --> 00:27:31.107
Well, I haven't started this season yet.

00:27:31.107 --> 00:27:32.233
It's been too cold.

00:27:32.233 --> 00:27:34.611
- But the EPA lives on.

00:27:34.611 --> 00:27:36.488
- Oh yeah.
- Okay, good.

00:27:36.488 --> 00:27:37.947
(both laughing)

00:27:37.947 --> 00:27:39.866
And where did you meet Harvey?

00:27:39.866 --> 00:27:42.744
Obviously Harvey Lewis
is our connective tissue,

00:27:42.744 --> 00:27:44.162
he introduced us.

00:27:45.497 --> 00:27:47.040
I know that you guys
are really good friends.

00:27:47.040 --> 00:27:48.708
When did you meet Harvey?

00:27:48.708 --> 00:27:50.502
- I'm not sure,

00:27:50.502 --> 00:27:54.381
but it was within the last four years.

00:27:55.507 --> 00:27:58.301
And I was very taken with him,

00:27:59.803 --> 00:28:02.931
I thought this is a tremendous athlete

00:28:04.557 --> 00:28:05.809
and a tremendous person.

00:28:07.185 --> 00:28:10.897
And he asked me twice
to speak to his class,

00:28:10.897 --> 00:28:13.149
his high school class.

00:28:13.149 --> 00:28:15.735
I said, "What are you teaching?"

00:28:15.735 --> 00:28:20.156
He said, "Well, government
and operation of cities

00:28:20.156 --> 00:28:22.325
"and that sort of thing."

00:28:22.325 --> 00:28:23.743
It's the high school kids.

00:28:24.619 --> 00:28:26.287
I said, "What I wanted to tell them

00:28:26.287 --> 00:28:31.292
"and this is about the
diet and athletic stuff,

00:28:32.127 --> 00:28:35.588
"because that would be what
they might be interested in."

00:28:36.923 --> 00:28:41.928
Harvey asked me every year if I wanna run

00:28:42.011 --> 00:28:45.974
the Flying Pig Marathon. (laughs)

00:28:47.267 --> 00:28:51.938
"5K or 10K?" (laughs)

00:28:51.938 --> 00:28:55.775
I'm not up to a marathon anymore.

00:28:55.775 --> 00:28:57.944
Unless somebody were to say,

00:28:57.944 --> 00:29:02.949
"Well, we'll give you $30,000
for charitable purposes."

00:29:06.703 --> 00:29:10.039
If he had said that, I would say,

00:29:10.039 --> 00:29:15.003
"I will train for a year to
run a marathon at this age,

00:29:16.129 --> 00:29:18.965
"if you gimme enough money to fund this

00:29:18.965 --> 00:29:20.675
"or that or the other."

00:29:20.675 --> 00:29:21.551
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:29:21.551 --> 00:29:22.719
- Nobody's taken me up on it.

00:29:22.719 --> 00:29:24.429
- I could see that happening though.

00:29:24.429 --> 00:29:27.974
Has anybody run a marathon at age 100?

00:29:27.974 --> 00:29:30.518
I don't think that that's ever occurred.

00:29:30.518 --> 00:29:31.936
- I don't think so.

00:29:31.936 --> 00:29:33.313
I really don't think so.

00:29:34.189 --> 00:29:37.400
I think I could, because I think

00:29:40.403 --> 00:29:41.237
a marathon,

00:29:44.073 --> 00:29:49.078
the one I set the world record in

00:29:51.331 --> 00:29:53.875
was not overly stressing.

00:29:54.876 --> 00:29:55.960
- It wasn't?

00:29:55.960 --> 00:29:58.630
- No, I think I could have gone further.

00:29:58.630 --> 00:29:59.464
- Really?

00:30:00.507 --> 00:30:03.968
I would imagine somebody
could put together some funds

00:30:03.968 --> 00:30:08.181
that would go to charity to see
you train and do a marathon.

00:30:09.390 --> 00:30:11.142
- Well, I would compromise.

00:30:11.142 --> 00:30:14.437
I would have to make a major
sacrifice in lifestyle.

00:30:14.437 --> 00:30:15.980
I think. (laughs)

00:30:15.980 --> 00:30:17.232
- (laughs) Yeah. I would imagine.

00:30:17.232 --> 00:30:18.691
- It might be possible.

00:30:18.691 --> 00:30:23.071
Maybe I'd find it wasn't
possible for me to do that.

00:30:23.071 --> 00:30:24.322
But, I would only do that

00:30:26.407 --> 00:30:30.036
if my training told me that I could.

00:30:30.036 --> 00:30:31.996
- Right. But you're doing,

00:30:31.996 --> 00:30:36.125
like, so recently you've
done five Ks with Harvey.

00:30:36.125 --> 00:30:38.753
So you're still doing
races, just not marathons?

00:30:38.753 --> 00:30:42.632
- He runs at my speed. (laughs)

00:30:42.632 --> 00:30:45.051
- He's an amazing human
being, that Harvey.

00:30:45.051 --> 00:30:46.803
So did you go and talk to the students?

00:30:46.803 --> 00:30:48.054
What did you tell them?

00:30:48.054 --> 00:30:51.182
- I told them about global warming

00:30:53.142 --> 00:30:58.147
and sustainability of
the world on this planet.

00:31:03.570 --> 00:31:08.575
And the reason I did that is
I thought through introducing,

00:31:10.535 --> 00:31:15.540
as best I can, the impact of diet,

00:31:17.375 --> 00:31:20.920
which translates to
agriculture in general,

00:31:20.920 --> 00:31:24.382
not completely because
we have to grow cotton

00:31:24.382 --> 00:31:27.093
and we have to grow trees.

00:31:27.969 --> 00:31:32.974
And that agriculture at
the time was supposed to be

00:31:33.433 --> 00:31:36.769
contributing, agriculture
and quotation marks,

00:31:37.896 --> 00:31:41.983
was supposed to be
contributing 51% of the CO2,

00:31:43.318 --> 00:31:48.281
the excess that we have in the atmosphere.

00:31:49.824 --> 00:31:53.870
41%, not 51%.

00:31:53.870 --> 00:31:55.622
And we have to reduce that.

00:31:59.792 --> 00:32:03.296
And somebody who understands
it can help inform the public

00:32:03.296 --> 00:32:08.092
and make, get the politics
in such a way as to do that.

00:32:08.092 --> 00:32:11.012
We have not been very
effective doing that.

00:32:11.012 --> 00:32:11.846
- No, we haven't.

00:32:11.846 --> 00:32:15.183
- We should have been
much, much more effective.

00:32:15.183 --> 00:32:20.146
By talking to you so that
other people can hear,

00:32:20.146 --> 00:32:24.776
is an important move
for me in my small way,

00:32:24.776 --> 00:32:29.781
and us, we have a little
share, we call it a think tank

00:32:29.948 --> 00:32:33.826
of four people at home
who meet once a month

00:32:35.286 --> 00:32:37.038
trying to get this word out.

00:32:38.081 --> 00:32:40.249
And we've done our own study

00:32:40.249 --> 00:32:44.379
about the effect of food alone

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:50.551
in agriculture of the
total CO2 equivalent,

00:32:50.551 --> 00:32:55.556
which includes some methane,
some NO2, NO, I mean,

00:32:56.057 --> 00:32:58.893
and water vapor to a certain extent.

00:32:59.978 --> 00:33:02.021
Those are the greenhouse gases..

00:33:02.021 --> 00:33:03.314
- Sure, sure, sure.

00:33:03.314 --> 00:33:06.526
Yeah. So climate change, sustainability,

00:33:06.526 --> 00:33:09.821
the impact of agriculture
on climate change,

00:33:09.821 --> 00:33:14.784
and also the ability to
be part of this solution

00:33:14.784 --> 00:33:16.619
by getting people to change their diet.

00:33:16.619 --> 00:33:19.038
This is something that
is important to you.

00:33:19.038 --> 00:33:21.457
You've been advocating
for this for a long time.

00:33:21.457 --> 00:33:24.502
And I know that you've put
a lot of time and energy

00:33:24.502 --> 00:33:26.921
and resources into restoring and improving

00:33:26.921 --> 00:33:29.507
Ohio's rivers and streams, right.

00:33:29.507 --> 00:33:32.385
You've been active in the restoration

00:33:32.385 --> 00:33:35.888
of the waterways in Ohio over the years?

00:33:35.888 --> 00:33:40.268
- Oh, that was before
the global warming issue.

00:33:40.268 --> 00:33:41.102
Yeah.

00:33:44.397 --> 00:33:48.192
I guess I was a canoe racer.

00:33:48.192 --> 00:33:50.611
I've been a canoe racer 60 years.

00:33:51.696 --> 00:33:53.364
I know how to run a canoe.

00:33:56.409 --> 00:33:58.619
In the early days,

00:34:00.788 --> 00:34:04.876
we worried about where we
paddled and it was degraded

00:34:04.876 --> 00:34:07.045
or there were dams in the way,

00:34:08.171 --> 00:34:12.425
or all sorts of nasty things
being done to the waters

00:34:13.301 --> 00:34:14.469
of the country.

00:34:15.595 --> 00:34:18.639
And they passed in 1968,

00:34:22.351 --> 00:34:27.356
the National Wild and Scenic
Rivers Act as a national thing.

00:34:27.523 --> 00:34:28.900
And the little Miami River,

00:34:28.900 --> 00:34:33.821
which goes through Cincinnati
was one of the first 27 rivers

00:34:34.906 --> 00:34:39.368
in the whole country designated
to become a candidate

00:34:39.368 --> 00:34:41.287
to be a member of the

00:34:41.287 --> 00:34:44.791
National Wild and Scenic Rivers system.

00:34:44.791 --> 00:34:49.420
And so that put a focus
on the little Miami River,

00:34:49.420 --> 00:34:52.548
which is a beautiful river,
and where I used to practice

00:34:52.548 --> 00:34:55.009
before I practiced on the lake.

00:34:55.009 --> 00:34:58.387
I was pretty far away, and
the reason I gave it up

00:34:58.387 --> 00:35:02.850
for the lake is because
it was not convenient.

00:35:04.685 --> 00:35:06.729
But I used to practice on that.

00:35:08.397 --> 00:35:13.402
The State of Ohio

00:35:14.195 --> 00:35:16.030
had a friendly governor

00:35:16.030 --> 00:35:20.743
who happened at the time to be a Democrat,

00:35:22.703 --> 00:35:24.247
and his name was John Gilligan.

00:35:25.748 --> 00:35:30.169
And he was sympathetic
to what we wanted to do.

00:35:30.169 --> 00:35:32.588
We wanted to protect Ohio's rivers.

00:35:32.588 --> 00:35:36.342
As a matter of fact, we
had set up an Ohio river,

00:35:39.428 --> 00:35:43.391
Ohio scenic river system a year before

00:35:43.391 --> 00:35:44.934
the federal government did,

00:35:48.020 --> 00:35:51.274
- The idea, at least the
idea that we had was that

00:35:52.191 --> 00:35:55.278
the people who really cared
about the rivers should be

00:35:55.278 --> 00:35:59.115
members of a board of a
statewide river protection group.

00:36:00.032 --> 00:36:02.743
And that's what we started with,

00:36:02.743 --> 00:36:07.748
and we were able through that
means to gather larger numbers

00:36:07.790 --> 00:36:09.041
of people together,

00:36:09.041 --> 00:36:12.086
get decisions made to
protect these rivers.

00:36:12.086 --> 00:36:17.091
And interestingly to me, anyway,

00:36:17.133 --> 00:36:22.138
we decided that the American
people have a way of expressing

00:36:23.222 --> 00:36:27.310
their interest in things
is measured in dollars.

00:36:28.519 --> 00:36:32.732
And I think we're internationally
known for that. (laughs)

00:36:34.400 --> 00:36:35.234
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:36:35.234 --> 00:36:39.363
- And we decided that the
little Miami River was bringing

00:36:39.363 --> 00:36:44.368
unbelievable returns to
the villages along it,

00:36:45.828 --> 00:36:50.166
economic returns to the canoe liveries,

00:36:51.083 --> 00:36:55.129
from the fishing, from the swimming,

00:36:55.129 --> 00:36:57.465
from the property value appreciation

00:36:57.465 --> 00:37:00.218
of having this beautiful stream go by,

00:37:00.218 --> 00:37:02.511
that the little children could play in.

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:09.560
And we decided it was bringing
in to the public $100,000

00:37:10.895 --> 00:37:13.856
a year, every year reliably,

00:37:13.856 --> 00:37:18.236
as well as employing these
kids who could hustle

00:37:18.236 --> 00:37:19.487
canoes in liveries,

00:37:22.406 --> 00:37:25.576
that would be hard a job to replace.

00:37:28.955 --> 00:37:32.917
And that meant

00:37:34.669 --> 00:37:39.674
$10 million a year for the
100 miles of that stream.

00:37:40.007 --> 00:37:43.678
100 miles of the little
Miami River would bring in

00:37:43.678 --> 00:37:47.223
$10 million a year just
because it was there.

00:37:47.223 --> 00:37:51.936
- Sure. So rather than appealing
to somebody's sensitivity

00:37:51.936 --> 00:37:53.396
to environmental issues,

00:37:53.396 --> 00:37:56.983
by focusing on the economic impact

00:37:56.983 --> 00:37:59.110
and appealing to the incentives

00:37:59.986 --> 00:38:01.862
of that to keep the rivers clean,

00:38:01.862 --> 00:38:03.447
that's what moves the needle.

00:38:03.447 --> 00:38:05.324
That's what actually-
- Exactly.

00:38:07.702 --> 00:38:11.747
Then we had a governor
whose name was Bob Taft,

00:38:13.124 --> 00:38:18.129
who was the son of, or
grandson of William Howard Taft

00:38:18.254 --> 00:38:21.799
of great national fame.
- Sure.

00:38:22.758 --> 00:38:27.722
- And I went to school with two Taft guys,

00:38:29.056 --> 00:38:30.474
they're both gone now,

00:38:35.479 --> 00:38:39.191
and Taft's wife, Hope,

00:38:40.985 --> 00:38:45.614
and he bought a place on
the Little Miami River

00:38:45.614 --> 00:38:50.619
to retire in after he was
retired by the voters years ago,

00:38:54.248 --> 00:38:57.585
And she got really excited
about water quality

00:38:57.585 --> 00:39:01.839
and the Little Miami River,
the upper 43 miles of it,

00:39:02.715 --> 00:39:03.841
it's a 100 miles total,

00:39:05.051 --> 00:39:07.595
and they lived in the upper 43.

00:39:11.474 --> 00:39:13.100
And with the Taft money and all

00:39:13.100 --> 00:39:17.521
there was no trouble to
get Ohio State University,

00:39:17.521 --> 00:39:20.983
which I had worked with for 12 years,

00:39:20.983 --> 00:39:24.737
to understand the economics
of the Little Miami River.

00:39:26.947 --> 00:39:30.951
And she got the guy
who was handed the job,

00:39:30.951 --> 00:39:35.456
after the professor I
worked with retired in 2008,

00:39:36.499 --> 00:39:39.919
Brent Sohngen his name
is, and they came out

00:39:39.919 --> 00:39:44.924
with a super, super economic
study, which is very simple.

00:39:47.802 --> 00:39:50.471
I haven't really had a
chance to study it yet.

00:39:52.598 --> 00:39:57.603
They were bringing in
something like $233,000 a year,

00:39:57.937 --> 00:40:02.942
year after year, instead
of $100,000 per mile,

00:40:04.151 --> 00:40:05.945
per mile of the river.

00:40:08.114 --> 00:40:09.490
Just economic value.

00:40:09.490 --> 00:40:11.534
- Right. So the numbers bore it out

00:40:11.534 --> 00:40:14.578
that it's in everyone's interest
to keep this river clean.

00:40:14.578 --> 00:40:19.125
And that advocacy led to
that level of preservation.

00:40:19.125 --> 00:40:20.084
Yeah. That's beautiful.

00:40:20.084 --> 00:40:21.961
- People spend money on it, you know?

00:40:23.087 --> 00:40:25.005
The first time we would put somebody

00:40:25.005 --> 00:40:27.550
that it's got a trail along it,

00:40:27.550 --> 00:40:29.718
and lots of people use that trail,

00:40:29.718 --> 00:40:32.221
it goes through different
counties and everything.

00:40:33.347 --> 00:40:37.601
And they can estimate how much,

00:40:37.601 --> 00:40:39.854
you put a guy on the trail,

00:40:39.854 --> 00:40:42.773
"Excuse me, you wanna take a survey,

00:40:42.773 --> 00:40:45.401
"see how you like the river
and how much you spend,

00:40:45.401 --> 00:40:48.112
"you spend on gas, you spend on a motel,

00:40:48.112 --> 00:40:51.198
"you spend on a bicycle,
you spend on skates,

00:40:51.198 --> 00:40:54.869
"you spend on clothes, you spend on food,

00:40:55.703 --> 00:40:57.496
"how much do you spend a day?"

00:40:57.496 --> 00:41:02.501
We figured out that they spend
about $20 a day per person.

00:41:04.670 --> 00:41:07.423
And I don't know how that's changed,

00:41:07.423 --> 00:41:09.258
I haven't read this new study yet.

00:41:09.258 --> 00:41:10.468
It just came out.

00:41:13.012 --> 00:41:16.724
But it's gonna be a national prize winner

00:41:16.724 --> 00:41:19.602
of an analysis of how much rivers

00:41:19.602 --> 00:41:21.520
are really worth in dollars.

00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:23.022
- Right, right, right, right.

00:41:23.022 --> 00:41:24.190
Yeah. That's super interesting-

00:41:24.190 --> 00:41:25.608
- So I'm proud, I'm really proud of that

00:41:25.608 --> 00:41:27.401
because we started that thing.

00:41:29.153 --> 00:41:32.072
- When you reflect back on your life,

00:41:33.199 --> 00:41:34.950
what is it that you,

00:41:34.950 --> 00:41:37.995
like the wisdom that
you accrue as somebody

00:41:37.995 --> 00:41:39.497
who's been alive for a long time,

00:41:39.497 --> 00:41:42.958
like what is the message
that you would like to impart

00:41:42.958 --> 00:41:46.754
to younger people about what
is important and what isn't?

00:41:46.754 --> 00:41:51.300
- Well, the conclusion
I came to long, long ago

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:57.348
was the real satisfactions
that people can get

00:41:57.348 --> 00:42:02.311
in their lifetimes and that they consist

00:42:04.146 --> 00:42:08.275
in helping other people, period.

00:42:08.275 --> 00:42:11.946
In whatever way, as much as you can.

00:42:12.947 --> 00:42:15.533
That brings real rewards.

00:42:17.451 --> 00:42:20.246
- Yeah. Service.

00:42:20.246 --> 00:42:23.457
- And yes, we're all servants

00:42:23.457 --> 00:42:27.378
and we should be, it brings the pleasures.

00:42:27.378 --> 00:42:28.671
It's not the money.

00:42:29.547 --> 00:42:31.966
If you've got money and
you're happy, good luck.

00:42:33.884 --> 00:42:37.888
- And so your service comes in the form of

00:42:37.888 --> 00:42:42.893
your environmental work,
this focus on climate change.

00:42:43.644 --> 00:42:48.023
What would be another example
of how you channel that

00:42:48.023 --> 00:42:51.443
service for others that has
given your life meaning?

00:42:53.112 --> 00:42:56.740
- Well, I think this is
my profession, as it were,

00:42:58.284 --> 00:43:01.161
the time I can spare
and it's a lot, I read,

00:43:03.914 --> 00:43:06.166
there's a list of books
I brought here today.

00:43:09.545 --> 00:43:10.879
I'm desperate to read these things.

00:43:10.879 --> 00:43:14.550
The latest one is advertised
in the "New York Times"

00:43:15.467 --> 00:43:18.596
Sunday review of books,

00:43:21.181 --> 00:43:23.434
and it talks about sustainability.

00:43:29.440 --> 00:43:31.609
And I need that book, I wanna see it.

00:43:34.820 --> 00:43:37.781
And the four of us in
our little think tank

00:43:37.781 --> 00:43:39.283
wanna understand that.

00:43:40.659 --> 00:43:44.288
The sustainability issue
is an enormous issue

00:43:45.289 --> 00:43:50.252
because there isn't any way
we can go from a population

00:43:56.800 --> 00:43:58.761
of close to 2 billion

00:44:00.888 --> 00:44:05.893
to one that is close to
8 billion in my lifetime,

00:44:08.228 --> 00:44:10.022
less than my lifetime,

00:44:12.358 --> 00:44:14.401
I think it was about 2 1/2 billion

00:44:14.401 --> 00:44:18.614
when I graduated from college in 1943,

00:44:21.784 --> 00:44:23.535
and now it's 8 billion.

00:44:23.535 --> 00:44:24.536
How do you do this

00:44:25.621 --> 00:44:27.623
on a planet is not getting any bigger?

00:44:27.623 --> 00:44:29.541
You can't farm anymore.

00:44:29.541 --> 00:44:31.210
You can't farm enough.

00:44:31.210 --> 00:44:33.587
We can't feed all the people now.

00:44:33.587 --> 00:44:35.297
It just won't work.

00:44:35.297 --> 00:44:37.132
We're finding it won't work.

00:44:38.050 --> 00:44:40.010
We're finding it in hunger.

00:44:40.010 --> 00:44:45.015
We're finding it in wars
because people get flooded out

00:44:45.015 --> 00:44:47.935
because the temperature
change is making the ground

00:44:50.020 --> 00:44:52.898
unavailable to produce food.

00:44:52.898 --> 00:44:57.486
The oceans are finding it
impossible to produce fish,

00:44:57.486 --> 00:45:02.032
relatively speaking here and
there, it's totally serious.

00:45:04.076 --> 00:45:08.205
So the sustainability factor
is every bit as important

00:45:08.205 --> 00:45:09.498
as the temperature,

00:45:12.751 --> 00:45:14.253
the world temperature rise.

00:45:17.589 --> 00:45:21.593
- So you're somebody who, you're
a child of the Depression.

00:45:21.593 --> 00:45:25.514
You lived through World
War II as a young person.

00:45:27.725 --> 00:45:30.227
When you kind of look back on your life

00:45:30.227 --> 00:45:34.022
and reflect upon your peers,

00:45:34.022 --> 00:45:37.860
I mean, we live in a society
where it is about high stress,

00:45:37.860 --> 00:45:42.364
it's about accumulation of
material things, but most people

00:45:42.364 --> 00:45:47.369
by the time they reach around
80 end up in, you know,

00:45:47.411 --> 00:45:51.707
facilitated living centers,
nursing homes, et cetera.

00:45:51.707 --> 00:45:54.126
You've been able to opt out of that.

00:45:54.126 --> 00:45:58.172
So aside from the service
piece and the diet,

00:45:58.172 --> 00:46:02.134
like what are the other
secrets of longevity

00:46:02.134 --> 00:46:05.304
that you've relied on that
people should know more about?

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:10.309
- Exercise. (laughs)
- Yeah.

00:46:10.309 --> 00:46:11.351
Breaking world marathon records.

00:46:11.351 --> 00:46:13.645
- Not necessarily related to diet.

00:46:13.645 --> 00:46:17.816
- Exercise, being of service, diet.

00:46:17.816 --> 00:46:21.111
What about friendships, community?

00:46:21.111 --> 00:46:21.945
- Absolutely.

00:46:21.945 --> 00:46:23.322
- Relationships.
- Absolutely

00:46:23.322 --> 00:46:25.699
- [Rich] Talk a little bit about that.

00:46:25.699 --> 00:46:26.784
- Absolutely.

00:46:28.202 --> 00:46:29.703
Call it love if you wanna.

00:46:31.955 --> 00:46:36.960
Well, I guess I'm succeeding
at that without trying.

00:46:38.420 --> 00:46:41.924
Became 100, I got a box
this big, birthday cards.

00:46:45.052 --> 00:46:46.804
- Wow. That's amazing.

00:46:46.804 --> 00:46:50.349
Most people as they get older
they have very few friends,

00:46:50.349 --> 00:46:52.226
but you have lots and lots of friends.

00:46:53.852 --> 00:46:56.814
- Well, you find you had friends
and you didn't realize it.

00:46:59.608 --> 00:47:03.362
I even got a citation
by the governor of Ohio.

00:47:04.780 --> 00:47:06.615
I didn't vote for him ever.

00:47:06.615 --> 00:47:10.118
- [Rich] (laughing) Okay.

00:47:13.872 --> 00:47:15.082
- As a matter of fact,

00:47:18.252 --> 00:47:20.128
the best governor we ever had

00:47:23.298 --> 00:47:26.426
married my third wife (laughs)

00:47:27.761 --> 00:47:29.388
and then he died.
- Okay.

00:47:30.681 --> 00:47:32.808
- I don't know. I'm very, very,

00:47:32.808 --> 00:47:37.771
I realize how tremendously
fortunate I happen to be.

00:47:41.024 --> 00:47:45.821
And it seems like it's
been a simple process,

00:47:45.821 --> 00:47:47.781
no intellectual adapter,

00:47:51.952 --> 00:47:56.415
dangerous ventures or anything,
but just grinding along

00:47:56.415 --> 00:47:57.916
doing the best you can.

00:48:02.045 --> 00:48:03.755
I've been very happy about it.

00:48:05.090 --> 00:48:09.344
- But staying engaged with
life with other human beings,

00:48:09.344 --> 00:48:13.098
having a sense of purpose, right.

00:48:14.099 --> 00:48:16.268
Like your connection to
climate change issues

00:48:16.268 --> 00:48:19.396
and sustainability, your love for running,

00:48:19.396 --> 00:48:21.773
and canoeing, and exercising.

00:48:21.773 --> 00:48:26.778
I would imagine these are big,
big pieces in what, you know,

00:48:26.862 --> 00:48:28.780
kind of gets you excited and out of bed

00:48:28.780 --> 00:48:29.990
in the morning still.

00:48:30.949 --> 00:48:35.287
- Well, we're all kind of
limited in what we can do

00:48:35.287 --> 00:48:40.083
because we feel, many of us anyway,

00:48:40.083 --> 00:48:42.210
that we're under the control of

00:48:43.587 --> 00:48:45.839
the ultra rich and corporations,

00:48:45.839 --> 00:48:49.509
and we're threatened in every respect

00:48:50.427 --> 00:48:51.887
when you come to think of it.

00:48:53.180 --> 00:48:56.475
And how you handle this,
because the corporations

00:48:57.768 --> 00:49:01.813
are the key to our financial success,

00:49:02.689 --> 00:49:06.944
but I leave that for
other people to work on.

00:49:06.944 --> 00:49:08.195
- (laughing) Yeah.

00:49:08.195 --> 00:49:11.198
Well, what is your, you know, your message

00:49:12.282 --> 00:49:15.369
to, like imagine there's
a lot of younger people

00:49:15.369 --> 00:49:16.995
who are listening to this,

00:49:16.995 --> 00:49:20.290
they want the wisdom of
somebody who has, you know,

00:49:20.290 --> 00:49:25.295
lived a life like yours;
service, friendships, purpose,

00:49:27.381 --> 00:49:29.841
what else do you wanna say to those people

00:49:31.176 --> 00:49:34.137
about how to live a fulfilling,
meaningful, long life?

00:49:35.847 --> 00:49:39.434
- Well, I think an approach to young men

00:49:39.434 --> 00:49:44.439
and a lot of young women now is sports.

00:49:48.527 --> 00:49:51.905
And the success of vegan
athletes is tremendous.

00:49:53.240 --> 00:49:57.703
Whole magazines are devoted
to it several times a year.

00:49:57.703 --> 00:49:58.704
And I see it.

00:49:59.913 --> 00:50:01.456
It never used to be that way.

00:50:03.500 --> 00:50:07.170
And you get kidded for eating special,

00:50:09.965 --> 00:50:12.384
"Who do you think you are?" (laughs)

00:50:17.681 --> 00:50:19.307
That's why I had some fun

00:50:19.307 --> 00:50:23.353
talking to Harvey's class. (laughs)

00:50:23.353 --> 00:50:24.896
- Well, Harvey's such a great ambassador

00:50:24.896 --> 00:50:25.856
of that as well.
- Yes he is.

00:50:25.856 --> 00:50:27.816
- So I imagine his students already know

00:50:27.816 --> 00:50:29.860
that he's a plant-based athlete.

00:50:29.860 --> 00:50:31.987
They know that he runs these crazy races,

00:50:31.987 --> 00:50:34.948
but probably receptive
to what you had to say.

00:50:34.948 --> 00:50:39.953
- I went shopping with him
at a Whole Foods Market

00:50:40.370 --> 00:50:43.999
in Cincinnati when he was
getting supplies for his

00:50:43.999 --> 00:50:48.712
Appalachian trail race.
- Oh, Appalachian trail.

00:50:51.131 --> 00:50:54.843
- And his father would
carry it in their van

00:50:56.303 --> 00:50:59.931
and cook for him when he came
in, and he's sleeping there,

00:50:59.931 --> 00:51:04.186
and then he'd go off the next
day and his father's (laughs)

00:51:04.186 --> 00:51:06.438
hundreds of miles away. (laughs).

00:51:09.316 --> 00:51:13.904
49 days to go 2,000 plus miles.

00:51:15.489 --> 00:51:18.158
The mere fact that a human can do that

00:51:19.076 --> 00:51:21.995
says that they understood the principles

00:51:21.995 --> 00:51:27.000
of feeding and resting and
limits to your output as well.

00:51:29.628 --> 00:51:31.296
And he's got it figure it out.

00:51:32.339 --> 00:51:33.173
It's beautiful.

00:51:34.549 --> 00:51:39.221
- Have you heard of the term Blue Zones?

00:51:39.221 --> 00:51:40.847
The Blue Zones people?

00:51:40.847 --> 00:51:43.058
Pockets of populations around the world-

00:51:43.058 --> 00:51:43.892
- Oh, yeah okay.
- Where people

00:51:43.892 --> 00:51:44.893
live the longest.
- It's coming back.

00:51:44.893 --> 00:51:46.061
- Yeah, centenarians.

00:51:46.061 --> 00:51:50.440
And they studied these
populations and they extracted

00:51:50.440 --> 00:51:55.445
from that certain tenets
or principles of lifestyle

00:51:55.904 --> 00:51:58.031
that all of these communities shared,

00:51:59.574 --> 00:52:03.161
among them are diets that are
predominantly plant-based,

00:52:03.161 --> 00:52:04.913
they aren't exclusively plant-based,

00:52:04.913 --> 00:52:07.791
but most of these
populations where they have

00:52:07.791 --> 00:52:09.751
the highest concentration of people living

00:52:09.751 --> 00:52:13.046
to 100 and beyond are eating
mostly a plant-based diet.

00:52:13.046 --> 00:52:18.051
But they also are populations
where they're moving

00:52:18.343 --> 00:52:20.387
all the time, they're not going to the gym

00:52:20.387 --> 00:52:22.180
or running marathons, but they have-

00:52:22.180 --> 00:52:23.265
- Seasonal.
- Yeah,

00:52:23.265 --> 00:52:25.600
movement-oriented lifestyles.

00:52:26.893 --> 00:52:31.898
And they have rich, rich
relationships within the community.

00:52:32.774 --> 00:52:36.695
So they have support and
they have connection.

00:52:36.695 --> 00:52:40.240
They also tend to be
relatively faith-based

00:52:40.240 --> 00:52:43.243
in different ways, like
faith is a big piece of it.

00:52:43.243 --> 00:52:46.204
And this idea of purpose,
like what, in Okinawa,

00:52:46.204 --> 00:52:50.500
they call ikigai, like
feeling like you have a reason

00:52:52.127 --> 00:52:52.961
for your living.

00:52:52.961 --> 00:52:56.339
And what I see in you is
an example of all of those

00:52:56.339 --> 00:52:58.133
principles in your life.

00:52:58.133 --> 00:53:00.552
- It is admirable what they do.

00:53:01.386 --> 00:53:04.848
I haven't been exposed to it.

00:53:04.848 --> 00:53:09.561
I know that they do exist, I
read a little bit about them,

00:53:10.562 --> 00:53:13.148
but there's always some
limit to what they can do

00:53:13.148 --> 00:53:15.317
because of this or that
or the other thing,

00:53:15.317 --> 00:53:19.154
but they do live longer,
they're are happy people.

00:53:20.238 --> 00:53:24.492
And maybe there are a lot
of ideas that they have

00:53:24.492 --> 00:53:27.913
that we could use if we
are smart enough to do so.

00:53:28.830 --> 00:53:30.290
- Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

00:53:30.290 --> 00:53:32.792
But the fact that you
didn't even kick into

00:53:32.792 --> 00:53:36.379
your highest gear in running
until you were around 60,

00:53:36.379 --> 00:53:39.174
and that's when you kind of really found

00:53:39.174 --> 00:53:41.176
your competitive groove with all of this.

00:53:41.176 --> 00:53:43.011
Like, it's very inspiring

00:53:43.011 --> 00:53:45.138
because that's a period
of time where most people

00:53:45.138 --> 00:53:48.350
would feel like it's time to slow down.

00:53:48.350 --> 00:53:51.311
And in your example,
that's where I feel like

00:53:51.311 --> 00:53:54.022
you really started to get
more engaged with your life.

00:53:56.149 --> 00:54:01.154
- I think, I was in business
long enough to understand

00:54:01.571 --> 00:54:04.074
that there's a competition

00:54:06.201 --> 00:54:08.662
that's normal to business

00:54:08.662 --> 00:54:11.331
in the United States and other countries.

00:54:11.331 --> 00:54:14.251
And so there's a spirit of competition,

00:54:14.251 --> 00:54:16.253
that's the way you get things done.

00:54:17.379 --> 00:54:20.340
And it's not totally

00:54:22.217 --> 00:54:27.222
compatible with the
lifestyle you would advocate,

00:54:28.848 --> 00:54:29.849
you might say.

00:54:33.478 --> 00:54:35.855
That's something I don't
know more about. (laughs)

00:54:35.855 --> 00:54:39.734
- Yeah. But you said that now
you feel like you're having

00:54:39.734 --> 00:54:41.444
the best time of your life.

00:54:43.196 --> 00:54:44.030
Yes?

00:54:45.282 --> 00:54:50.120
- Well, we had a birthday
party on the beach in Florida.

00:54:51.496 --> 00:54:53.415
- I saw the video.

00:54:53.415 --> 00:54:55.125
There's a video on the internet of that,

00:54:55.125 --> 00:54:57.168
of your birthday party.

00:54:57.168 --> 00:55:02.173
- I have a nephew who was
the mayor of Vero Beach,

00:55:02.674 --> 00:55:07.679
which is where we have
relatives and where we, (laughs)

00:55:09.306 --> 00:55:13.518
and he called up somebody
who used to be manager

00:55:13.518 --> 00:55:18.481
of the local newspaper, "The
Vero Beach Press Journal"

00:55:20.859 --> 00:55:24.612
and he came out on the beach
to see me on my birthday

00:55:24.612 --> 00:55:26.197
and took all our stuff down,

00:55:29.492 --> 00:55:30.452
and I published it the next day.

00:55:30.452 --> 00:55:32.871
And Marilyn and I went around
and it fetched 10 copies

00:55:32.871 --> 00:55:35.040
of the newspapers so we could send it

00:55:35.040 --> 00:55:37.792
to our families. (laughs)

00:55:38.668 --> 00:55:39.502
- And you're here in Los Angeles,

00:55:39.502 --> 00:55:42.172
you have children, grandchildren,

00:55:42.172 --> 00:55:45.592
and a great-granddaughter
here that you have yet to meet

00:55:45.592 --> 00:55:46.718
because of the pandemic.

00:55:46.718 --> 00:55:47.552
Is that right?

00:55:49.095 --> 00:55:52.724
- I haven't met her because
it's been questionable

00:55:52.724 --> 00:55:54.434
whether one should fly.

00:55:55.393 --> 00:55:56.227
- Well, you're here.

00:55:56.227 --> 00:55:58.355
So you're gonna have a big family reunion?

00:55:59.939 --> 00:56:02.359
- Yeah, we will. We will.

00:56:02.359 --> 00:56:07.072
So that's, Marilyn's sister
lives in Lake Arrowhead

00:56:09.240 --> 00:56:11.576
upper hill in San Bernardino.

00:56:14.037 --> 00:56:19.042
And she's gonna see us
this afternoon. (chuckles)

00:56:19.042 --> 00:56:19.876
- Good.

00:56:21.836 --> 00:56:25.590
Well, I wanna kind of end this

00:56:29.594 --> 00:56:31.971
on a note of inspiration.

00:56:31.971 --> 00:56:35.350
Like, again, going back
to looking over the course

00:56:35.350 --> 00:56:39.354
of your life, you've lived
through so many periods

00:56:40.271 --> 00:56:41.439
of American history,

00:56:42.524 --> 00:56:44.567
and, you know, now
we're, as you mentioned,

00:56:44.567 --> 00:56:47.779
tipping into 8 billion
people on the planet.

00:56:47.779 --> 00:56:51.408
Like when you reflect back
over the course of your life,

00:56:51.408 --> 00:56:55.787
like how do you feel about
the future of America

00:56:55.787 --> 00:56:58.873
and the future of the
planet going forward?

00:57:00.542 --> 00:57:02.502
- Well, either we face a period

00:57:02.502 --> 00:57:07.424
of really tremendous
suffering, massive suffering,

00:57:09.384 --> 00:57:13.638
or we get smart enough
to do some important

00:57:15.890 --> 00:57:20.520
laws and work on them and understand them

00:57:22.105 --> 00:57:27.110
so that we can continue to
manage this little bitty planet.

00:57:28.903 --> 00:57:31.781
We can't, you can talk
about going to Mars,

00:57:31.781 --> 00:57:34.284
I don't know anybody that'd
be willing to do that.

00:57:36.202 --> 00:57:37.537
Who's gonna write the newspaper?

00:57:37.537 --> 00:57:40.081
(both laughing)

00:57:40.081 --> 00:57:42.000
Who's gonna run the grocery store?

00:57:42.000 --> 00:57:45.420
Just one of, you know-
- Mars? Are you kidding.

00:57:45.420 --> 00:57:46.588
- We'll see. We'll see.

00:57:46.588 --> 00:57:49.966
But yeah, sure, let's save
our planet in the meantime,

00:57:49.966 --> 00:57:53.386
rather than abandon it
for another, of course.

00:57:53.386 --> 00:57:54.512
- Oh, I don't know.

00:57:54.512 --> 00:57:58.183
There may be one out there out
of the billions or trillions

00:57:58.183 --> 00:58:01.102
of planets that there are. (laughs)

00:58:01.102 --> 00:58:03.229
- Right. The fact that you have thrived

00:58:03.229 --> 00:58:05.482
on a plant-based diet over these years,

00:58:05.482 --> 00:58:09.861
you're an incredible example of the health

00:58:09.861 --> 00:58:12.447
and robustness of eating this way.

00:58:12.447 --> 00:58:14.532
And the fact that you've
set these world records

00:58:14.532 --> 00:58:17.952
eating this way, you're an
early pioneer of this lifestyle.

00:58:17.952 --> 00:58:20.163
I know that you're friends

00:58:20.163 --> 00:58:23.625
with a lot of the legendary
doctors in this space,

00:58:23.625 --> 00:58:28.630
Neal Barnard, and Colin
Campbell, and Caldwell Esselstyn.

00:58:28.796 --> 00:58:33.051
And I just, I salute you
for being an ambassador

00:58:33.051 --> 00:58:37.472
and like such a youthful energy exemplar

00:58:38.932 --> 00:58:41.476
of eating a plant-based diet and being

00:58:41.476 --> 00:58:44.604
this incredible athlete along the way.

00:58:44.604 --> 00:58:47.524
And I really do think that
maybe we should try to see

00:58:47.524 --> 00:58:49.192
if you can get that marathon in.

00:58:50.026 --> 00:58:51.569
- It's a wonderful thing.

00:58:51.569 --> 00:58:54.489
It's a quality of life
issues, as a matter of fact,

00:58:55.490 --> 00:58:58.284
if people are nice to
you when you get old.

00:58:58.284 --> 00:58:59.661
(both laughing)

00:58:59.661 --> 00:59:02.997
- Well, it's a privilege to
be able to speak to somebody

00:59:04.999 --> 00:59:08.753
who has been around for a
while and has wisdom to impart.

00:59:08.753 --> 00:59:10.922
- Well, yeah, I have been there,

00:59:10.922 --> 00:59:13.424
I've been through this and
I've been through that,

00:59:14.801 --> 00:59:18.596
And I'm fortunate, very
fortunate that I survived.

00:59:18.596 --> 00:59:23.226
My father died of liver
cancer and he had six months

00:59:23.226 --> 00:59:27.814
of terrible pain and he'd been an athlete,

00:59:27.814 --> 00:59:31.859
he'd been a gymnast
when he was in college.

00:59:35.446 --> 00:59:37.657
Diet was standard American.

00:59:38.616 --> 00:59:41.786
But liver cancer, I'm sure
it was caused by diet.

00:59:42.704 --> 00:59:43.913
I'm sure of it.

00:59:43.913 --> 00:59:45.290
- [Rich] Right.

00:59:45.290 --> 00:59:47.166
- Today we would've fixed him up.

00:59:49.377 --> 00:59:52.380
But he died at the age of 69

00:59:55.341 --> 01:00:00.346
and my mother died of a heart
attack, which was standard,

01:00:01.431 --> 01:00:03.933
you sort of expect it when
they're in their '70s.

01:00:06.436 --> 01:00:09.897
Could have probably prevented
that if I'd known then

01:00:09.897 --> 01:00:11.024
what I know now.

01:00:11.024 --> 01:00:16.029
- Sure. But your blood work
is good, you're healthy,

01:00:16.195 --> 01:00:17.989
you're gonna be around for a while.

01:00:17.989 --> 01:00:18.823
- I hope so.

01:00:18.823 --> 01:00:19.657
- Everything's good.

01:00:23.036 --> 01:00:23.870
You go to the doctor,
you get your checkups.

01:00:23.870 --> 01:00:25.705
Everything is a-okay?

01:00:25.705 --> 01:00:26.581
- Yes.

01:00:37.258 --> 01:00:38.509
They called me and asked me to come in

01:00:38.509 --> 01:00:43.014
to have a regular test.

01:00:43.014 --> 01:00:45.892
And I did, and I passed it
with flying colors (laughs).

01:00:45.892 --> 01:00:48.061
- You did. Amazing.

01:00:48.061 --> 01:00:49.771
(both laughing)

01:00:49.771 --> 01:00:53.191
Are the doctors surprised
when you come in?

01:00:54.567 --> 01:00:56.694
Or they know you now,
like, "This is just Mike."

01:00:56.694 --> 01:00:58.988
- Well, it's only one or two,

01:00:58.988 --> 01:01:00.823
when you come right down to it.

01:01:00.823 --> 01:01:01.783
(both laughing)

01:01:01.783 --> 01:01:02.950
- [Rich] Yeah.

01:01:05.119 --> 01:01:05.953
- They have to accept it

01:01:05.953 --> 01:01:10.708
because I can challenge
them too easily. (laughs)

01:01:12.001 --> 01:01:13.503
- Right. Right, right.
- Anyway-

01:01:13.503 --> 01:01:15.713
- Thank you for coming
to talk to me today.

01:01:16.547 --> 01:01:20.426
You are an inspiration and
it is amazing, the fact

01:01:20.426 --> 01:01:24.097
that you change your diet at 70.

01:01:24.097 --> 01:01:26.641
- This is probably not a question,

01:01:27.975 --> 01:01:32.647
it's actually the high point
of what you may call my career,

01:01:34.482 --> 01:01:37.318
to be interviewed by
you who has interviewed,

01:01:38.361 --> 01:01:41.406
I got your Volume II,
it's a gorgeous thing.

01:01:41.406 --> 01:01:43.074
- Oh, you did? Good, good.

01:01:43.074 --> 01:01:44.867
- It weighs about 20 pounds.

01:01:46.411 --> 01:01:47.245
- You can use curls.

01:01:47.245 --> 01:01:48.079
with it.
- It's gorgeous,

01:01:48.079 --> 01:01:50.665
where did you get the
photography like that?

01:01:51.541 --> 01:01:54.419
All those people who were so inspiring.

01:01:57.046 --> 01:01:59.799
(chuckles) That's not my department.

01:01:59.799 --> 01:02:03.594
- No, I think what you're
doing is incredibly inspiring,

01:02:03.594 --> 01:02:05.680
and your example, I think,

01:02:05.680 --> 01:02:09.392
is very uplifting to
everybody who would hear this

01:02:09.392 --> 01:02:12.895
or see this who's thinking
about their future

01:02:12.895 --> 01:02:16.649
and what that might look
like as we all get older.

01:02:16.649 --> 01:02:17.775
And, you know, again,

01:02:17.775 --> 01:02:20.278
I've said it many times over
the course of our conversation,

01:02:20.278 --> 01:02:25.283
but the fact that you have
remained so, not only active,

01:02:26.659 --> 01:02:30.538
but really intentional in
how you're living your life

01:02:30.538 --> 01:02:33.458
is, I think, a real
inspiration to all of us.

01:02:33.458 --> 01:02:35.543
And so I guess just in closing,

01:02:35.543 --> 01:02:38.880
if there's any kind of
final thoughts or wisdom

01:02:38.880 --> 01:02:41.215
that you would like to impart to anybody

01:02:41.215 --> 01:02:42.633
who's listening to this.

01:02:42.633 --> 01:02:47.638
- Well, I'm very grateful
for this opportunity.

01:02:47.638 --> 01:02:50.099
My gang of four will be very pleased

01:02:50.975 --> 01:02:54.520
that I was able to express
myself on this and that subject

01:02:54.520 --> 01:02:58.107
that we work on once a month normally.

01:03:00.693 --> 01:03:03.780
And that's a major impact I could have

01:03:03.780 --> 01:03:06.449
is to reach other people
with this message,

01:03:07.575 --> 01:03:11.871
because I'm saying it
works for me, big time,

01:03:13.164 --> 01:03:17.877
to be at least 22 years
older than the average person

01:03:17.877 --> 01:03:20.755
who dies in America.

01:03:23.841 --> 01:03:27.386
And I feel that's not
long enough yet. (laughs)

01:03:27.386 --> 01:03:31.432
- Yeah. I think you've got a
lot of life left in you, Mike.

01:03:31.432 --> 01:03:33.726
Yeah. So you're always welcome here.

01:03:33.726 --> 01:03:36.062
And I look forward to hanging
out with you for a little bit

01:03:36.062 --> 01:03:37.522
after we're done here.

01:03:37.522 --> 01:03:40.274
And I just appreciate you, sir.

01:03:40.274 --> 01:03:41.484
So thank you.

01:03:41.484 --> 01:03:42.985
And keep doing what you're doing,

01:03:42.985 --> 01:03:45.446
I think it's important
and powerful, like I said.

01:03:46.739 --> 01:03:49.992
And just to be a thriving
example of a plant-based diet,

01:03:49.992 --> 01:03:52.286
it's a wonderful, wonderful thing.

01:03:52.286 --> 01:03:54.121
- Thank you.
- Yeah. You're very welcome.

01:03:54.121 --> 01:03:56.666
- From the bottom of
my heart, I thank you.

01:03:56.666 --> 01:03:58.251
- Right back at you, my friend.

01:04:00.169 --> 01:04:03.130
(light outro music)

